## 1. Introduction
Sodium ion battery (SIB), a most potential alternative of lithium-ion batteries (LIBs) for the storage of renewable energy, has drawn increasing attention owing to the abundance and low price of sodium resource on earth shell [1–3]. However, the sluggish electrochemical kinetics and large volumetric expansion in the anode caused by the inherent large radius $(1.02\mathring{\mathsf{A}})$ as well as molar mass of sodium ion $\left(\mathsf{N a}^{+}\right)$ still hinder the practical application of the SIBs [4,5]. In addition, the large ${\mathsf{N a}}^{+}$ ionic radius could also more easily cause the polarization of electrode during the cycling process, resulting in poor cycle stability and bad rate performance. Therefore, it is essential and desirable to explore more effective and reasonable strategy to tackle these issues for further promoting the commercialization of SIBs.
Currently, transition metal sulfides (TMSs) have been widely regarded as the appealing anode candidates for SIBs because of their prominent theoretical reversible capacities, high electrical conductivity, rich redox reactions, good mechanical and thermal stability [6,7]. Particularly, the cobalt-based sulfide material, as a typical p-type semiconductor, has been extensively exploited as the anode material for SIBs owing to the naturally abundant reserve, cost effectiveness, good safety and high theoretical capacity [8,9]. Nevertheless, the single-component metal sulfides always have poor electronic conductivity and low electrochemical activity, leading to poor cycling stability and gradient degradation performance during the intercalation/deintercalation of ${\mathsf{N a}}^{+}$ ion process, thereby limiting their large-scale applications. To overcome the above-mentioned issues and maximize the energy storage advantages of TMSs, multiphase construction, namely combining different components of TMSs, is an efficient and controllable route to enhance the electrochemical performance since the synergistic effect between the different components could result in the pseudo-capacitance effect with the inhibition of those side reactions. Meanwhile, the phase boundaries at the heterointerfaces of the multiphase materials due to the difference of potential and lattice structure could offer rich defects, distortions and lattice mismatches, greatly affecting the charge carrier transport behavior and the internal electric field at the heterointerfaces since the different bandgaps of the dissimilar coupling components would also significantly promote the electron/ion transport and the interfacial reaction kinetics [10–12]. For instance, Cao et al. [13] fabricated a porous hollow MnS- $\cdot(Z\mathrm{nCo})\mathrm{S}/\mathrm{N-C}$ composite, which showed good cycling stability with a capacity of $393\ \mathrm{mA}\mathrm{h}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ within 400 cycles at $1.0\mathsf{A}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ but poor rate performance. Luo et al. [14] successfully obtained (Ni, Fe, $\mathsf{C o})\mathsf{S}_{\mathbf{x}}$ nanocrystals locked in N-doped carbon nano-cubes with more channels for the diffusion of ions, however, the fabrication required relatively high annealing temperatures. Therefore, it is necessary to rationally design and optimize the multiphase TMSs with different compositions as well as unique structure for achieving the satisfactory electrochemical performance.
Recently, metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) with large specific surface area as well as pore volume, tunable pore size, uniform ordered pores and tunable chemical composition are considered as a universal template or precursor to fabricate various TMSs [15–18]. In the present work, tri-metal-organic frameworks (CoNi-Zn MOFs) were firstly designed and employed as the precursor to synthesize CoNiZnS material embedded into the carbon matrix (CoNiZnS/C) with a microsphere structure through a facile solid sulfidation method. As the anode material for SIBs, the obtained $\mathsf{C o N i Z n S/C}$ composite exhibited good electrochemical performance. Particularly, the optimum composite named as CoNiZnS/ C-2 in this work showed an excellent initial Coulombic efficiency of $96.52\%$ at $100\mathrm{mAg}^{-1}$ with long-term cyclic performance (maintaining $410.7\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{h}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ after 960 cycles at $2\mathsf{A}\mathsf{g}^{-1}.$ and rate capability (with a capacity of $477.0\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{h}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at a high current density of $5\mathrm{A}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ ).
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1. Synthesis of Co-Ni-Zn MOFs
The Co-Ni-Zn MOFs was synthesized by a facile solvothermal method. In a typical synthesis of Co-Ni-Zn (100-200-40) MOFs, $C_{0}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ ( $\mathrm{100mg}^{\cdot}$ , ${\sf N i}({\sf N O}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6{\sf H}_{2}0$ $\cdot200\mathrm{mg},$ and $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6-$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ ( $\mathrm{40~mg)}$ were dissolved in ethylene glycol $\mathrm{:}15~\mathrm{mL}$ with ultrasonic treatment (solution A). P-benzenedicarboxylic acid ${\bf\cdot90}~\mathrm{mg}$ $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{B D C},$ was mixed with N, N-dimethylformamide $\cdot24\mathrm{\mL,DMF},$ 1 in ultrasonic bath (solution B). Then, solutions A and B were mixed and stirred for about $^\textrm{\scriptsize1h}$ at the ambient temperature. Thereafter, the mixture was introduced into a Teflon-lined sealed stainless steel autoclave $(50~\mathrm{mL})$ and kept at $150^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $^\textrm{\scriptsize6h}$ . After cooled down to the ambient temperature, the pale-pink precipitation was collected by centrifugation and washed by ethanol and DMF successively thoroughly, and finally dried at $60~^{\circ}C$ in a vacuum oven for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ . For comparison, other two kinds of Co-Ni-Zn MOFs were prepared by following the same process as the above with different weight ratios of $C_{0}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ and ${\sf N i}({\sf N O}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6{\sf H}_{2}0$ i.e., 1:1, 2:1, and labelled as Co-Ni-Zn (150-150-40) MOFs and Co-NiZn (200-100-40) MOFs, respectively. In addition, the Co-Ni-Zn (100-200-x) MOFs prepared with different masses of $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6-$ $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}$ , Co-Zn MOFs and Zn MOFs were also obtained using the same process. The morphology characterizations of the obtained Co-NiZn (100-200-x) MOFs, Co-Zn MOFs and Zn MOFs are described in the supporting materials (see Figs. S1 and S3a, b).
