## 2.3. Characterizations
Crystalline structure and phase purity were characterized on an X-ray powder diffractometer (XRD, Smartlab 9 kW, Rigaku, Japan) with the radiation source of $\mathsf{C u}\ \mathsf{K}\mathsf{\mathfrak{a}}$ $\langle\lambda=1.5418\mathrm{~\AA~}$ ). Scanning electron microscope (SEM, SU8010, Hitachi, Japan) at an operating voltage of $15.0\ \mathrm{kV}$ and the high-resolution transmission electron microscope (TEM, JEM-2100F, JEOL, Japan) with an accelerating voltage of $200\ \mathrm{kV}$ were employed to measure the morphology and microstructure. The NOVA 4200e instrument was applied to record the nitrogen adsorption-desorption isotherm curve at $77~\mathrm{K}$ to obtain the Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) specific surface area and Barret-Joyner-Halenda (BJH) pore size distribution. Surface elemental compositions and chemical states were collected by an X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy analysis (XPS) (VG ESCALAB 250, Thermo Electron, UK) using Al $\mathsf{K}\mathsf{\mathfrak{A}}$ as the $\mathsf{X}$ -ray source (1486 eV).
## 2.4. Electrochemical analysis
For the fabrication of working electrode, $75\mathrm{\Deltawt\%}$ of the asobtained sample as the active material was mixed with $15\mathrm{\wt\%}$ conductive Super P powder and $10\mathrm{{wt\%}}$ sodium carboxymethylcellulose (CMC) to form a homogeneous slurry, which was coated onto a copper foil. The obtained copper foil was dried overnight in a vacuum oven at $80^{\circ}C$ and then cut into several pieces (diameter: $12~\mathrm{mm}$ ) and used as the working electrode. Herein, the loading amount of the active material was ${\sim}1.2~\mathrm{mg~cm}^{-2}$ . A sodium metal foil, a glass fiber sheet and a solution by dissolving of ${\mathsf{N a S O}}_{3}$ - ${\mathrm{CF}}_{3}$ $1.0\mathrm{\mol\L}^{-1},$ ) in diglyme (DGM) were served as the counter electrode, the separator and the electrolyte, respectively. The coin cell was assembled in an Ar-filled glove box. The galvanostatic charge/discharge cycling performance was measured by a battery test system (LAND CT2001A model, Wuhan LAND Electronic Co. Ltd.) at different current densities over $0.3\substack{-3.0\mathrm{V}}$ range. The electrochemical workstation operated at $0.1\mathrm{mV}s^{-1}$ was used to record cyclic voltammetry (CV) curve. Electrochemical impedance spectrum (EIS) collected in $0.01~\mathrm{Hz}\mathrm{-}100~\mathrm{kHz}$ frequency range was also obtained by the electrochemical workstation.
## 3. Results and discussion
The process for synthesis of CoNiZnS/C composite is schematically described in Fig. 1. As stated above, the pink Co-Ni-Zn MOFs microspheres precursor was fabricated via the solvothermal method by the coordination of $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{B D C}$ organic functional group with $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}/\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}/\mathrm{Co}^{2+}$ . To investigate the growth pattern of microsphere morphology, a series of experiments with different weights of $C_{0}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ , ${\sf N i}({\sf N O}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6{\sf H}_{2}0$ and $Z_{\mathrm{{n}(N O_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6H_{2}O}}$ were conducted. Figure S1 exhibits SEM images of different $C_{0}{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{N i}{\mathrm{-}}Z\mathsf{n}$ (100-200-x) MOFs prepared with different masses of $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6{\cdot}$ - $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}$ , i.e., 0, 10, 20, 30, 40, and $50~\mathrm{mg}$ . In the absence of $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6-$ $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}$ , a lumpy morphology without any microspheres was found (Fig. S1a). With the mass increasing, the microsphere structure became more and more uniform (Figs. S1b, c, d and e). Especially, when the mass was increased to $40~\mathrm{mg}$ , the Co-Ni-Zn (100-200- 40) MOFs demonstrated uniform hollow microsphere structure with a larger size of ${\sim}2$ um (Fig. S1e). While, some microsphere structures seemed to be broken in the case using $50~\mathrm{mg}$ of Zn $(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ (Fig. S1f). Therefore, the growth of microsphere structure could be controllably obtained by tuning $Z_{\mathrm{{n}(N O_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6H_{2}O}}$ addition amount. Furthermore, SEM images of other Co-Ni-Zn MOFs prepared with different weight ratios of $C_{0}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ and Ni $(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ , i.e., 1:1, 2:1, namely Co-Ni-Zn (150-150-40) MOFS and Co-Ni-Zn (200-100-40) MOFs are shown in Fig. S2. Their morphologies were almost the same as that of Co-Ni-Zn (100-200-40) MOFs. Subsequently, these Co-Ni-Zn MOFs were served as the precursor to form trimetallic sulfides embedded into the carbon matrix $({\mathsf{C o N i Z n S}}/{\mathsf{C}})$ through the solid sulfurization by using thioacetamide (TAA) as the sulfur source.
As depicted in Figs. 2a and 2b, the resultant CoNiZnS/C-2 composite demonstrated typical sea urchin-like morphology with a size of approximately 2 um. From the low-magnification TEM image (Fig. 2c), the hollow sea urchin-like microstructure was observed. While, in the HRTEM images (Figs. 2d, 2e, 2f and 2g), the lattice spacing of $0.235\ \mathrm{nm}$ was near to either that of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ $0.236\ \mathrm{nm}$ , JCPDS Card No. 42-1448) or $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}S_{4}$ $\cdot0.237\ \mathrm{nm}$ , JCPDS Card No. 43-1469), corresponding to (400) facet of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ or (004) facet of $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}S_{4}$ ; the lattice fringe with an interplanar dspacing of $0.545\ \mathrm{nm}$ was precisely indexed to either the (111) plane of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ $0.55~\mathrm{\nm}$ , JCPDS Card No. 42-1448) or $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ $\langle0.548\ \mathrm{nm}$ , JCPDS Card No. 43-1469) [19,20]; the interplanar spacing of $0.31~\mathrm{{nm}}$ was assigned to the (111) plane of ZnS $.0.312~\mathrm{{nm}}$ JCPDS Card No. 05-0566) [21]. In addition, as displayed in Fig. 2h, the elemental mapping obviously revealed that C, Co, Ni, S, and Zn elements co-existed with uniform distributions, indicating the successful synthesis of CoNiZnS/C-2 composite. Fig. S3 shows the morphologies of $Z{\mathrm{nS}}/C$ , CoZnS/C, CoNiZnS/C-1 and CoNiZnS/C-3 composites, in which some different morphologies were observed. Figs. S4 and S5 show TEM images of CoNiZnS/C-1 and CoNiZnS/C-3 composites, in which both composites also demonstrated the sea urchin-like morphology with hollow structure as the CoNiZnS/C2 composite.
To identify the crystal structure and phase purity, XRD analysis was performed. As depicted in Fig. 3a, CoNiZnS/C-1, CoNiZnS/C-2 and CoNiZnS/C-3 composites showed similar XRD pattern, in which the main diffraction peaks at $2\uptheta\ =\ 16.16^{\circ}$ , $2\uptheta\:=\:26.58^{\circ}$ , $2\uptheta=31.32\ensuremath{^\circ}$ , $2\uptheta=37.98^{\circ}$ , $2\uptheta=47.27\mathrm{\textperthousand}$ , $2\uptheta=50.22\mathrm{\uprho}$ and $2\uptheta=55.00^{\circ}$ were well-indexed to (111), (220), (311), (400), (422), (511) and (440) facets of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ (JCPDS Card No. 42-1448) as well as (111), (022), (113), (004), (224), (115) and (044) planes of $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}S_{4}$ (JCPDS Card No. 43-1469), respectively [22,23], while the peaks at $2\uptheta\:=\:28.55^{\circ}$ , $2\uptheta\ =\ 47.51^{\circ}$ and $2\uptheta\ =\ 56.28^{\circ}$ were fully assigned to (111), (220), and (311) planes of ZnS (JCPDS Card No. 05-0566). These XRD analysis results are consistent with the HRTEM analysis results (Figs. $2\mathrm{d}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{\cdot}$ . Similarly, for the $\scriptstyle{\cos/\mathsf{C}}_{0}Z_{\mathrm{n}}\varsigma/C$ composite, the diffraction peaks corresponded to those of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ (JCPDS Card No. 42-1448) as well as ZnS (JCPDS Card No. 05- 0566) (Fig. S6), and for $z{\mathrm{nS}}/{\mathrm{C}}$ composite, the diffraction peaks (Fig. S6) matched well with the standard ZnS (JCPDS Card No. 05- 0566).
Figs. 3b and 3c show the nitrogen adsorption-desorption isotherm measurement results for the CoNiZnS/C-1, CoNiZnS/C-2 and CoNiZnS/C-3 samples. As depicted in Fig. 3b, all the curves exhibited the typical type-IV isotherms with a hysteresis loop, indicating the existence of mesoporous structure [24–26]. While, the specific surface areas of the CoNiZnS/C-1, CoNiZnS/C-2 and CoNiZnS/C-3 samples were calculated to be about 69.8, 71.1 and $92.6~\mathrm{cm}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively. According to BJH calculation, the pore size distribution of these three samples displayed more pores with the average diameters of $3\mathrm{-}5~\mathrm{{nm}}$ (Fig. 3c). Such mesoporous structures could offer more contact areas with electrolyte, facilitating the sodium ion/electron transformation and mitigating the volumetric expansion during the charging/discharging process, thereby further improving the electrochemical performance. Thus, it is expected that such CoNiZnS/C composites could show excellent $\mathsf{N a}^{+}$ ions storage performance.
To further confirm the chemical compositions and corresponding elemental valence states of the CoNiZnS/C-1, CoNiZnS/C-2 and CoNiZnS/C-3 composites, the XPS analysis was carried out. As displayed in Fig. 3d, Co, Ni, Zn, S, O and C elements co-existed in the three composites. Herein, the existence of O should be due to the exposure of the samples in air, resulting in the surface oxidation. Based on the Gaussian fitting, the high-resolution XPS spectrum of $\mathtt{C o}\ 2\mathtt{p}$ can be divided into two clear spin-orbit doublet peaks and two shakeup satellites (named as ‘‘Sat.” in Fig. 3g). The spinorbital doublet peaks at 779.2 and $794.2\ \mathrm{eV}$ were ascribed to Co $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ and Co $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ of $C0^{3+}$ , respectively. While, the other spinorbital doublet peaks at 782.1 and $798.2\ \mathrm{eV}$ were related to Co $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ and Co $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ of $C o^{2+}$ , separately. These results indicated the co-existence of ${\dot{C}}0^{2+}$ and ${\mathsf{C o}}^{3+}$ . In addition, two satellite peaks were situated at 785.3 and $802.4~\mathrm{eV}$ , also belonging to Co $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ and Co $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ , respectively. Herein, the peaks of Co $\mathsf{2p}$ spectra including two spin-orbit doublets and shakeup satellites were consistent with the characteristic peaks of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{S}_{4},$ suggesting the presence of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ in the three samples [27]. The Ni 2p spectra (Fig. 3h) of the
CoNiZnS/C-2 sample can be deconvolved into four peaks positioned at 857.3, 861.7, 875.1 and $880.4~\mathrm{eV}$ , corresponding to $\mathsf{N i}^{3+}$ and satellite of Ni $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ and Ni $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ , respectively, which was in agreement with the reported literature [28]. Fig. 3e reflects the high-resolution $Z{\mathfrak{n}}\ 2{\mathfrak{p}}$ spectrum, which can be split into two prominent peaks at 1022.4 and $1045.7\ \mathrm{eV},$ assigning to Zn $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ and Zn $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ , respectively [29]. In the deconvoluted $\mathsf{S2p}$ spectrum (Fig. 3i), three peaks centered at 161.9, 163.3 and $164.8\ \mathrm{eV}$ were observed, which were corresponded to $\$2p_{3/2}$ and $\mathsf{S}2\mathsf{p}_{1/2}$ of $S^{2-}$ and bridging $S_{2}^{2-}$ ions in $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ , respectively [30]. Additionally, the C 1s spectrum (Fig. 3f) also showed three peaks at 284.7, 286.2 and $288.9\ \mathrm{eV}$ , ascribing to $\mathsf{s p}^{2}\mathsf{C}\mathsf{-C},\mathsf{s p}^{3}\mathsf{C}\mathsf{-C},$ and $\scriptstyle{C=0}$ separately, which were formed during the solid sulfurization [31,32].
Fig. 4a displays the initial forth cyclic voltammograms (CVs) of CoNiZnS/C-2 based electrode, which was tested at $0.1\ \mathrm{mV}\ s^{-1}$ in a $0.3\substack{-3.0\mathrm{~V~}}$ potential range. In the first scanning, three reduction peaks located at 0.7, 0.86 and $1.10\mathrm{~V~}$ were observed, relating to the stepwise sodiation reaction mechanism containing intercalation reaction forming the Na-rich phases, conversion reaction with the formation of metal element as well as ${\mathsf{N a}}_{2}{\mathsf{S}}_{\mathrm{i}}$ , and alloying reaction between Zn and Na, accompanying by the irreversible formation of the solid electrolyte interface (SEI) caused by the decomposition of the electrolyte. While, three oxidation peaks at
1.78, 1.97 and $2.14\mathrm{~V~}$ were also found, which were ascribed to the reversible oxidation reactions of metal elements into metal sulfides (e.g., $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ , $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}S_{4}$ , ZnS) [33–35]. In the following three cycles, two reduction peaks at around 0.91 and $1.40{\mathrm{V}}$ and two oxidation peaks at about 1.71 and $2.12\mathrm{~V~}$ appeared. Simultaneously, both reduction and oxidation peaks demonstrated stable and good reproducibility, indicating the satisfactory cycling reversibility. In addition, the initial forth cyclic voltammograms (CVs) of CoNiZnS/C-1 and CoNiZnS/C-3 based electrodes are shown in Fig. S7, which are similar with those of CoNiZnS/C-2 based one.
In order to evaluate the sodium storage performance, the galvanostatic charge/discharge measurements in $0.3–3\mathrm{~V~}$ at various current densities were conducted. Figs. 4b-d, S8a-b show the selected galvanostatic charge and discharge curves in the 1st, 2nd, 5th, 10th, and 20th cycles for the five anode materials at $100\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{\g}^{-1}$ . As shown in Fig. 4b, the initial discharge specific capacity of CoNiZnS/C-2 based anode was as high as $622.6\operatorname{mA}\mathrm{h}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ with a reversal charge specific capacity of $601.0\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{h}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , and an excelleny initial Coulombic efficiency (CE) of $96.52\%$ . Such a high initial CE could be resulted from the reversible SEI layer formation at the interface of ether-based electrolyte/electrode, benefitting from the microstructure and compositions of the material and/or the pseudo-capacitance effect [36,37]. By contrast, the initial charge/discharge capacities of $\mathrm{ZnS/C,\CoZnS/C,\CoNiZnS/C-1}$ and
CoNiZnS/C-3 based anodes were 289.7/420.3, 546.1/569.7, 571.4/605.7 and $586.7/615.7~\mathrm{mA}$ h $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ (see Figs. S8a, S8b, 4c and 4d), with the initial CEs of $68.92\%$ , $95.86\%$ , $94.34\%$ and $95.30\%$ , respectively, which are lower than that of CoNiZnS/C-2 based anode. The cycling performances of $z{\mathrm{nS}}/{\mathrm{C}}$ , CoZnS/C, CoNiZnS/C-1, $\mathsf{C o N i Z n S}/\mathsf{C}{-}2$ and CoNiZnS/C-3 based anodes for SIBs at 1 A $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ are displayed in Figs. 4e and S8c. It is apparent that the $\mathsf{C o N i Z n S}/$ C-2 based anode showed the excellent cycling performance with a specific capacity of $442.2\ \mathrm{mA}$ h $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ even after 400-cycle testing, which is much higher than those of $z{\mathrm{nS}}/{\mathrm{C}}$ $(27.4\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ ), $\scriptstyle{\cos/\mathsf{C}}_{0}Z_{\mathrm{n}}\varsigma/{\mathsf{C}}$ $\mathrm{(396.8\mA\h\g^{-1}}.$ , CoNiZnS/C-1 $390.1\ \mathrm{mA}\mathrm{h}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}.$ ) and CoNiZnS/C-3 $(412.7\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{h}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\cdot$ ) based anodes. The long-term cycling performance of CoNiZnS/C-2 anode was also carried out at $2\mathsf{A}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ . As displayed in Fig. 4f, this anode still delivered an excellent discharge specific capacity of $410.7~\mathrm{\mA}$ h $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ with a CE of $\sim100\%$ after the 960th cycle, which exhibited a higher capacity than most reported metal sulfides derived from MOFs. All these results clearly indicated that the CoNiZnS/C-2 based anode had superior cyclic stability, which should be attributed to its abundant phase boundaries and unique architecture. Additionally, Fig. $4\mathrm{g}$ exhibits the rate performances of
CoNiZnS/C-1, CoNiZnS/C-2 and $\mathsf{C o N i Z n S}/\mathsf{C}{-}3$ based anodes at vrious current densities. While, the rate capabilities of $z{\mathrm{nS}}/{\mathrm{C}}$ and $\scriptstyle{\cos/\mathsf{C}}_{0}Z_{\mathrm{n}}\varsigma/C$ based anodes at various current densities are displayed in Fig. S8d. Among the five anodes, the CoNiZnS/C-2 based one also showed the best rate performance. Taking CoNiZnS/C-2 based anode as an example, the rate capacity was investigated in details. It is found that this anode delivered the specific reversible capacities of 581.9, 552.9, 540.5, 529.5, 509.9, and $477.0\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{h}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at 0.1, 0.2, 0.5, 1, 2, and $5\mathsf{A}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ , respectively. Notably, the specific capacities of 496.4, 501.8, 500.2, 498.4, and $497.9\mathrm{\mA}$ h $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ were regained, separately, without significant reduction when the current densities were reversed back to 2, 1, 0.5, 0.2, and $0.1\mathrm{~A~g}^{-1}$ , suggesting the good reversibility and fast ion kinetics. Especially, these three electrode materials showed the special properties with small difference of capacities at various current densities. Only at $5\mathrm{~A~g~}^{-1}$ , there existed a relatively large difference of capacities among the three anode materials. That is, the capacity of CoNiZnS/C-2 based anode $(477.0\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{\h\g}^{-1})$ ) was the highest compared with those of CoNiZnS/C-3 ( $455.6\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{h}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1};$ and $\mathsf{C o N i Z n S}/$ C-1 $\mathsf{\Omega}^{\prime}431.5\mathrm{\mA\h\g}^{-1}.$ ) anodes. The SEM image of the CoNiZnS/C-2 anode after 100 cycles at $1\mathsf{A}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ are shown in the Fig. S9. The morphology of the CoNiZnS/C-2 anode maintained almost unchanged after 100 cycles, indicating the excellent structural stability of the electrode. The good rate performance was further confirmed by EIS measurement. Fig. 4h shows the Nyquist plots of the CoNiZnS/C-1, CoNiZnS/C-2, CoNiZnS/C-2, CoZnS/C and $z{\mathrm{nS}}/{\mathrm{C}}$ based electrodes after 50th full-discharged state at $0.2\mathrm{~A~g^{-1}}$ , in which each curve includes a compressed semicircle corresponding to the charge transfer resistance $\left(R_{\mathrm{ct}}\right)$ at the interface between electrode and electrolyte in the high frequency area and a sloping line in the low frequency area representing Warburg impedance $(Z_{\mathrm{w}})$ corresponding to the sodium ion diffusion in the electrode [38,39]. According to the fitting calculation, the $\mathbb{R}_{\mathrm{ct}}$ value of CoNiZnS/C-2 based anode $(0.85\Omega)$ was relatively lower than those of $\mathsf{C o N i Z n S}/\mathsf{C}_{-1}$ $\left(2.21~\Omega\right)$ , CoNiZnS/C-3 $\left(1.42\Omega\right)$ , CoZnS/C (3.71 X) and $z{\mathrm{nS}}/{\mathrm{C}}$ $_{(3.15~\Omega)}$ based ones (see Table S1), suggesting that $\mathsf{C o N i Z n S}/\mathsf{C}/-2$ based anode had excellent electronic conductivity. Additionally, to further comprehend the diffusion kinetics of ${\mathsf{N a}}^{+}$ ions, the diffusion coefficient (D) was quantitatively evaluated based on the following formulas [40,41]:
$$
Z_{w}=R_{S}+R_{c t}+\sigma_{w}\omega^{-1/2}
$$
$$
D=\frac{R^{2}T^{2}}{2A^{2}n^{4}F^{4}C^{2}\sigma_{w}^{2}}
$$
Warburg impedance factor $(\sigma_{\mathrm{w}})$ can be speculated from the slope (Fig. 4i), which is always obtained by the linear relationship between $Z_{w}$ and $\omega^{-1/2}$ according to the Eqn. (1). After 50-cycle, the $\sigma_{\mathrm{w}}$ value of the CoNiZnS/C-1, CoNiZnS/C-2, CoNiZnS/C-3, CoZnS/C and $z{\mathrm{nS}}/{\mathrm{C}}$ based electrodes were 37.14, 29.49, 29.79, 66.26 and 401.65, respectively, which indicated the faster diffusion of ${\mathsf{N a}}^{+}$ ions in the CoNiZnS/C-2 based electrode.
In order to further understand the reaction kinetics of CoNiZnS/ C-2 based electrode, the CV curves were measured at various sweep rates in the $0.1{-}1.5\ \mathrm{mV}\ s^{-1}$ range. As shown in Fig. S10b. all CV curves displayed almost the same shape at different scan rates, reflecting the electrochemical pseudo-capacitance feature. While, Figs. S10a, S10c and S11a display the original CV curves of CoNiZnS/C-1, CoNiZnS/C-3 and $\scriptstyle{\cos/\mathsf{C}}_{0}Z_{\mathrm{n}}\varsigma/C$ based electrodes. However, due to the existence of the irreversible polarization, with the increasing of the scanning rate in the CV curve, the anodic peak inevitably shifted to the positive whereas the cathodic peak slightly shifted to the negative [42]. Therefore, after the depolarization, namely potential calibration, the calibrated CV curves of CoNiZnS/C-1, CoNiZnS/C-2, CoNiZnS/C-3 and $\scriptstyle{\cos/\mathsf{C}}_{0}Z_{\mathrm{n}}\Sigma/\mathbf{C}$ based electrodes are displayed in Figs. 5a, 5d, 5g and S11b, respectively, where the peak positions at different scanning rates are nearly the same. Meanwhile, the pseudo-capacitive contributions to the overall current responses at different sweep rates in the calibrated CV curves can be quantified by [43,44]:
$$
i=k_{1}\nu+k_{2}\nu^{1/2}
$$
or
$$
\frac{i}{\nu^{1/2}}=k_{1}\nu^{1/2}+k_{2}
$$
Where, $k_{1}\nu$ stands for pseudocapacitive-controlled contribution, $k_{2}\nu^{1/2}$ represents diffusion-controlled contribution. According to the above calculations, the pseudocapacitive-controlled contribution at $1.5\mathrm{\mV\}\mathsf{s}^{-1}$ for CoNiZnS/C-2 based electrode reached to ${\sim}95.05\%$ , as shown in the red area of Fig. 5e, which is higher than those of $\mathsf{C o N i Z n S/C}–1$ $.\sim94.07\%$ , Fig. 5b), CoNiZnS/C-3 $(\sim94.82\%$ , Fig. 5h) and $\scriptstyle{\cos/\mathsf{C}}_{0}Z_{\mathrm{n}}\varsigma/C$ $\sim78.14\%$ , Fig. S11c) based electrodes, revealing good charge-transfer kinetics. Similarly, the proportion of the pseudocapacitive contributions for the CoNiZnS/C-2 based electrode at different sweep rates are displayed in Fig. 5f, in which the pseudocapacitive contribution was increased gradually with the increase of the scanning rate. Moreover, the capacitive contributions at 0.1, 0.3, 0.5, 0.7, 0.9, 1.2 and $1.5\mathrm{\mV\}\mathsf{s}^{-1}$ were 84.21, 87.16, 89.05, 90.62, 92.24, 94.48 and $95.05\%$ , respectively, reflecting that the pseudo-capacitance behavior should be dominant in the electrochemical reactions at the high scan rate and simultaneously suggesting the reason why the CoNiZnS/C-2 based electrode had excellent performance at high current densities. While, the capacitive contributions of the CoNiZnS/C-1 and CoNiZnS/C-3 based electrodes at different sweep rates are demonstrated in Figs. 5c and 5i, respectively. By comparison, the capacitive contributions of the $\scriptstyle{\cos/\mathsf{C}}_{0}Z_{\mathrm{n}}\Sigma/\mathsf{C}$ based electrode at various scan rates were also obtained by calculation. As displayed in Fig. S11d, they were relatively lower than those of the CoNiZnS/C-1, CoNiZnS/C-2 and CoNiZnS/C-3 based electrodes. Herein, the excellent performance should be attributed to the hollow sea urchin-like microsphere structure and advantageous compositions for the promoting of the ${\mathsf{N a}}^{+}$ ion transformation and alleviating of the volumetric expansion during the cycling.
## 4. Conclusions
In summary, the trimetallic sulfides (CoNiZnS) encapsulated in the carbon matrix $({\mathsf{C o N i Z n S}}/{\mathsf{C}})$ with a microsphere structure were successfully fabricated by a simple solid sulfidation method using the tri-metal-organic frameworks as the precursor. As they were employed as anode material for SIBs, the optimum one (denoted as $\mathsf{C o N i Z n S}/\mathsf{C}{-}2)$ showed an excellent initial Coulombic efficiency of $96.52\%$ at $0.1\mathrm{~A~g^{-1}}$ , excellent cyclic stability by maintaining a capacity of $410.7\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{h}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ even after 960th cycle at $2\mathrm{~A~g~}^{-1}$ and good rate performance with a capacity of $477.0\mathrm{\mA}$ h $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ at $5\mathsf{A}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ , which should be ascribed to its special microsphere structure with abundant phase boundaries. The proposed preparation strategy using the multi-metal-organic frameworks as the precursor is expected to become a feasible and promising one to design and develop the electrode materials for the high-performance secondary batteries.
